<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>9.10.7: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02495</md:content-id>
    <md:title>9.10.7: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>3560d2d7-8b1a-4b3e-8d7f-5b9c8d41e16c</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><emphasis><emphasis>
    <para>Students will complete the following questions to practice the skills they have learned in this lesson.</para>
  </emphasis></emphasis>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>The following quadratic expressions all define the same function.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \((x+5)(x+3)\) </item>
  <item> \(x^2+8x+15\) </item>
  <item> \((x+4)^2−1\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> statements that are true about the graph of this function.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> The \(y\)-intercept is \((0, -15)\). </item>
  <item> The vertex is \((-4, -1)\). </item>
  <item> The \(x\)-intercepts are \((-5, 0)\) and \((-3, 0)\). </item>
  <item> The \(x\)-intercepts are \((0, 5)\) and \((0, 3)\). </item>
  <item> The \(x\)-intercept is \((0, 15)\). </item>
  <item> The \(y\)-intercept is \((0, 15)\). </item>
  <item> The vertex is \((4, -1)\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The vertex is \((-4, -1)\), the \(x\)-intercepts are \((-5, 0)\) and \((-3, 0)\), the \(y\)-intercept is \((0, 15)\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>The following expressions all define the same quadratic function.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \((x−4)(x+6)\) </item>
  <item> \(x^2+2x−24\) </item>
  <item> \((x+1)^2−25\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the \(y\)-coordinate of the \(y\)-intercept \((0, \_\_\_)\) of the graph of the function?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -24</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item><emphasis>Select two</emphasis> \(x\)-intercepts of the graph.</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> \((6, 0)\) </item>
  <item> \((-4, 0)\) </item>
  <item> \((-6, 0)\) </item>
  <item> \((5, 0)\) </item>
  <item> \((4, 0)\) </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> \((-6, 0)\), \((4, 0)\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -1</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of the graph?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -25</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Is this an accurate graph of the function?</item>
</list>
<para><figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/9aec3a655cd4d57c085d5079858841326695259b"/></media></figure></para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> Yes </item>
  <item> No </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> Yes</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>Here is one way an expression in standard form is rewritten into vertex form.</item>
</list>
<para>Original expression</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 1</emphasis> - \(x^2−7x+(-\frac{7}{2})^2+6−(-\frac{7}{2})^2\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 2</emphasis> - \((x−\frac{7}{2})^2+6−\frac{49}{4}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 3</emphasis> - \((x−\frac{7}{2})^2+\frac{24}{4}−\frac{49}{4}\)</para>
<para><emphasis>Step 4</emphasis> - \((x−\frac{7}{2})^2-\frac{25}{4}\)</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>In Step 1, where did the number \(-\frac{7}{2}\) come from?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> It is the substitute value for \(x\). </item>
  <item> Every term in the expression was divided by 2. </item>
  <item> It is half of -7 being squared. </item>
  <item> Only the non-linear term was divided by 2. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> It is half of -7 being squared.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>In Step 1, why was \((-\frac{7}{2})^2\) added and then subtracted?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> It is added to complete the square. It is subtracted to keep the value of the expression unchanged. </item>
  <item> It is subtracted to complete the square. It is added to keep the value of the expression unchanged. </item>
  <item> It is added to complete the square. It is subtracted to change the value of the expression. </item>
  <item> It is subtracted to complete the square. It is added to change the value of the expression. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> It is added to complete the square. It is subtracted to keep the value of the expression unchanged.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What happened in Step 2?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> The expression is subtracted to keep the value of the expression unchanged. </item>
  <item> The expression, which is a perfect square, is written as a squared expression. </item>
  <item> The expression, which is not a perfect square, is written as a squared expression. </item>
  <item> The expression is added to keep the value of the expression unchanged. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The expression, which is a perfect square, is written as a squared expression.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>What happened in Step 3?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> The quadratic term is written as a fraction so that it has a common denominator, which makes it easier to subtract. </item>
  <item> A random term was added to complete the square. </item>
  <item> The linear term is written as a fraction so that it has a common denominator, which makes it easier to subtract. </item>
  <item> The constant term is written as a fraction so that it has a common denominator, which makes it easier to subtract. </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The constant term is written as a fraction so that it has a common denominator, which makes it easier to subtract.</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>What does the last expression tell us about the vertex of the graph of a function defined by this expression?</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent">
  <item> The vertex of the graph is at \((-6\frac{1}{4}, \frac{7}{2})\). </item>
  <item> The vertex of the graph is at \((\frac{5}{2}, -\frac{5}{2})\). </item>
  <item> The vertex of the graph is at \((\frac{7}{2}, -6\frac{1}{4})\). </item>
  <item> The vertex of the graph is at \((\frac{5}{2}, -6)\). </item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> The vertex of the graph is at \((\frac{7}{2}, -6\frac{1}{4})\).</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="4" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the missing number, including the sign, for the expression when \(d(x)=x^2+12x+36\) is written in vertex form.</item>
</list>
<para>\(d(x)=(x\_\_\_)^2\).</para>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> +6</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="5" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the missing numbers, including the signs, for the expression when \(f(x)=x^2+10x+21\) is written in vertex form. \(f(x)=(x + \underline{\;\mathbf a\boldsymbol.\;} )^2 \underline{\;\mathbf b\boldsymbol.\;}\)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in the place of the letter a?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> +5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, for the letter b?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="6" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the missing numbers, including the signs, for the expression when \(g(x)=2x^2−20x+32\) is written in vertex form. \(g(x)= \underline{\;\mathbf a\boldsymbol.\;} (x + \underline{\;\mathbf b\boldsymbol.\;} )^2 \underline{\;\mathbf c\boldsymbol.\;}\) </item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in place of letter a?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> +2</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in place of letter b?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -5</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in place of letter c?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -18</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="7" type="enumerated">
  <item>Enter the missing numbers, including the signs, for the expression when \(h(x)=(4x-3)^2\) is expanded to standard form. \(h(x)= ( \underline{\;\mathbf a\boldsymbol.\;}\; x )^2 + 2 ( \underline{\;\mathbf b\boldsymbol.\;}\; x)(-3) + ( \underline{\;\mathbf c\boldsymbol.\;} )^2\)</item>
</list>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in place of letter a?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> +4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in place of letter b?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> +4</para>
<list class="os-raise-noindent" start="3" type="enumerated">
  <item>What is the value of the missing number, including the sign, in place of letter c?</item>
</list>
<para><emphasis>Answer:</emphasis> -3</para></section>
</content>
